David Alan Gilbert wrote:
> 
> Stefan Schroepfer wrote:
> >
> > I found a security hole in my RH 4.2 system (164LX, Millenium II).
> >
> > In rare cases, while the screen is locked, pressing <Ctrl> and
> > then <Enter> gives me access to the system without entering a
> > password. I don't know how to reproduce this reliably but this
> > problem definitely exists.
> 
> When this happens do you get any errors or anything like that?
> Did it crash?

not evidently, no core file lying around
but: in /var/log/messages I find messages like (always the same
addresses):
---
Mar 31 11:42:39 ep194 kernel: xlock: memory violation at pc=15555e1cbf8
rp=15555e1c55c (bad address = 1201c6088)
---
+ every unauthenticated access (without typing a password) seems
  to lead to such a message
+ I'm not sure if every such message was caused by an
  unauthenticated access
+ this possibly only occures if I use -mode swirl

> Is the xlock process still running?

hmm, don't know, didn't look at it, having rebooted this morning
while the problem is not easily reproducible

> Does X keep running or does it ditch you out of X completely?

X and all other clients are alive.

As a general remark on xlock:
I know that disabling DontZap (by commenting it out in XF86Config) is
insecure because *anyone* could zap (quit X by pressing Crtl-Alt-BS)
while xlock is running (and so getting access to the system).
But the problem described here is another one.

