Hi !

> Well, I kinda ran into the wall after I wrote that message.  The card works
> great in the 21164sx I have, but if you EVER try to configure it.. you need
> a dos system. AND if you want to change the speeds of the card or any

This is a real problem and does not affect only intel cards...
It's fun with 3c509-cards too. I'm using TP here and gave my
Alpha to a customer using Coax for three weeks... 
First I've tried to patch the driver by overriding dev->if_port to 3 
(coax)... Ok, it doesn't work at all, so I needed a intel based machine.

The only linux-based setup tool out there is IMHO for old SMC/WD 8003 cards;
I've used it two years ago on i386-systems without problems.

Donald Brecker is able to read the NV-RAM of a card; why is it not possible
to write to it too ? A simple gerneric override like driver_{bnc,aui,tp}={0,1}
like the de620 driver does would also be really nice.

Another (easier?) solution may be to setup the card with the dos-based 
utility; all possible combinations (AUTO/BNC/AUI) and dump the EEPROM 
under DOS with a function like read_eeprom(...) in 3c509.c.
Under Linux only a write_eeprom(...) is then needed to upload the dumped
values. (If we diff the dumps, a nice setup-tool is possible too)

