On Sun, 5 Apr 1998, Robb Romans wrote:

> Finally, my machine came with 32MB of RAM, and I would like to upgrade it.
> I've searched the archives, but couldn't come up with a clear answer of the
> exact type of RAM that this machine needs to run Linux successfully. I hear
> that it needs 36 bit true parity SIMMS, but that it may not run without 
> "genuine DEC RAM." I sure as hell hope that isn't true, because DEC wants 
> about $1000 for 128 meg. Can someone straighten me out? 

I also bought an XL300 recently, but I have not installed Linux on it yet. 
The manual that came with my machine said that 36 bit true parity SIMMS were
the type of memory that is needed.  I do not recall reading anything about
genuine DEC memory.  The memory should be 70 ns or faster.  The manual also
said that each of the two banks of memory must be either full or empty. 
Since each bank consists of 4 slots, you must use memory in groups of 4
SIMMS.  I have purchased 4 16MB SIMMS to put in bank 1.  Bank 0 has 4 8MB
SIMMS, so the total will be 96MB.  I have not received the memory yet, so I
don't know for sure if it will work, but I will keep you informed.  It should
arrive on Monday or Tuesday. 

If you need memory at a good price, I would recommend Treasure Chest 
Computers (http://www.tccomputers.com).  They sell 16 MB true parity 
SIMMS for $42 each (plus shipping).  You might be able to get them a 
little cheaper from Onsale.com.

BTW, I  would like to comment that this is one of the best-built machines 
I have owned.  All components seem to be very rugged. I think it is a 
great machine for the price.

Regards,

Andy Seila

