On Mon, Apr 06, 1998 at 04:20:13PM +0200, Ralph Gillen wrote:
> I still have to call cdrecord with fs=0. It works fine, but ... what is the way 
> to get around this?

this is getting a FAQ.

The maximum size for one shm segment on Linux/Alpha is smaller than the shm
segment, which cdrecord requests. There are two ways to solve this problem,
either limit the size by giving cdrecord the fs option or by increasing
the allowed shm size in the kernel (change SHMMAX in asm-alpha/shmparam.h)

> Second question, a bit off-topic: cdda2wav reads audio tracks from my Toshiba 
> 6.7x with about 0.3x speed ... does anybody have experiences with newer 
> models/brands, error correction features etc ..?

AFAIK Toshiba reads cddr data only single speed (c't reported something like
that in their test about cddr capable cdroms). And for scratch elimination
cdda2wav reads overlapped blocks from the cd. So some of the data get read 
twice. Also the time for resynchronastion prior to the data already read,
will lead to the noticed low reading speed. You can increase it to nearly
single speed by turning off overlapped reading.

Thomas.

