Stephen,

Maybe I missed something, but are you trying to install Red Hat 5.0?  If
so, you should be installing a kernel from a floppy telling MILO
'root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1'.  You don't even use the hard disk when you
first boot the kernel on installation.  When I installed Red Hat 4.2 on my
noname box - same as UDB, ARC console, I don't recall that the scsi disks
were read at all when I first booted from the floppy.  They were
identified in the boot process, however.  Then, in the install process,
fdisk was used to create some Linux partitions and the appropriate Linux
files were copied to this partition.  After that, (almost) everything was
smooth sailing.  I put the program linload.exe in a fat-formatted
partition along with NT, so it would boot from the hard disk.  I don't
think Linux can read an NTFS formatted disk - maybe I'm wrong. 

Once again, maybe I misunderstood what you are trying to do, or you are 
using a different firmware from me.

Good luck.

Andy Seila
