	A quick question about the Alpha's CMOS, i.e. both ARC and SRM. Is
it possible to password protect these? With standard Intel x86 BIOSs, one
can set a password that is requried to access/modify the CMOS, but is not
needed to boot the machine. I looked around in ARC, but I could find no
such thing. Is there such a thing, and if so, where is it? This is for a
UDB 166MHz.
	If any one is wonder why I want to do this, it is just to secure
my UDB. On my intel boxes I have set the BIOS to boot only from the hard
drive, password protected the CMOS settings, and set the delay time on
lilo to zero. This keeps some one from booting my boxes with a linux
floppy boot disk or entering linux single mode. I have a firewall between
me and the internet, but I can't secure physical access to my machines
very well (in college dorm), so I figured this would be the next best
thing.
	Anyway, thanks in advance for any help anyone can give me.

PS. I know, someone could still pop out my HD and take them else where and
boot into them on another computer, so then I would need to encrypt the
drives and all that mess.... There is no such thing as ultimate security,
but it is stupid to pass up simple, easy security precautions! 

