On Tue, 7 Apr 1998, Aaron wrote:

> I've just recieved a new noname board with a 21066a processor on it (233)
> and am planning on putting together an Linux machine for my dad.  What
> power supply is reccomended.  I was told 250 or 300 watts.  Which is more
> appropriate.  300 watts I have to order a supply, but 250 I can get today,
> but I don't want to have trouble later on.  (I know that the UDB power    
> supplies are WOAFULLY inadequate if it got MILDLY warm outside) 
>  
> Ideas would be much appreciated.

I think 250 watts is plenty, unless you plan putting 3 or 4 BIG disks and 
other accessories in the system.  I built a system using the same board 
(axppci33, 233 mhz), and have 2 scsi disks, a scsi cdrom and a scsi tape, 
and it is running fine with a 250 watt power supply.

You might be careful in buying a case for the system.  I found that an 
old AT-style tower case I had would not fit because of the placement of 
the components on the board.  I don't remember the exact problem, but I 
had to buy a new case, and it had to be a full tower case in order to 
accomodate the board.  If you are thinking about ordering a case, I would 
recommend that you not do it, but take the motherboard to a local 
computer shop and try several cases to get one that fits the board and 
your needs.

Secondly, my cpu failed after 4 months, probably because of inadequate 
cooling.  I had two fans in the case, but one was positioned to blow 
across the top of the cpu heatsink.  You should make sure you have two 
fans (in addition to the fan in the power supply), and make sure the one 
in the front blows DIRECTLY THROUGH the fins of the cpu heatsink.  

Good luck!

Andy Seila

