He He!  That's a good inside joke!  In other words, don't try.  Only 3.5"
and smaller drives fit and 3.5" aren't even recommended b/c they restrict
precious air flow.  I'm not even sure if 

1) you can boot from it
2) that you can get Linux to use it.

You'd also need a mounting bracket and a 2mm to 0.100inch header
converter, plus a power adaptor.  Also, if you have a PCI slot, this
argument for 3.5" is useless:  There is simply no room.  If you must have
an internal, get an old SCSI laptop drive.  Maybe an IDE one works but I
don't know how to use it.  Can anyone help here?  There are separate
headers for SCSI and IDE, both have 2mm headers, SCSI also has 0.100 inch
headers. The only way I know to get cables for either is to buy ribbon and
connectors, say from Digikey, and snap your own.  This is more
electromechanical hacking than most people can or care to do.  The
mounting bracket for laptop hard drives were included with the machine
originally.

You could try starshipcorp.com and get most of this stuff in the form of a
kit, but they wanted more money than I saw fit.

