Ahem, the glibc-alpha update soap opera is getting a tad annoying, isn't
it? 

All the test belows give identical results with portmap-4.0-8.alpha.rpm
and portmap-4.0-7.alpha.rpm

1) glibc-2.0.6-9.alpha.rpm, the only one that *almost* works, doesn't let
   me login from a linux console (an "Access violation" message flashes after
   I enter a username at the login prompt, and I never get at the password
   prompt - we run NIS, so portmap is the chief suspect for this). X logins
   via xdm work fine.
   Also, I can't resolve *any* DNS name from an i386 application run via 
   em86, but the resolver works ok for native apps (so no netscape, which
   was the main reason for bothering with em86).

2) glibc-2.0.7-4.alpha.rpm completely nuked my system three weeks ago,
   and cost me a lenghty and tedious recovery. All dynamically linked
   apps segfaulted (ld-linux is the chief suspect).

3) glibc-2.0.7-6.alpha.rpm tried yesterday. Now I can login from a linux
   console as root only (root has an entry in /etc/passwd, unlike regular 
   users that go trhough NIS). But *all* NIS lookups fail with connection 
   refused from portmap. Before you ask, yes, I looked into hosts.allow, 
   I even tried "portmap: ALL : ALLOW", thank you.
   The glibc interaction with portmap is obviously still broken, since the
   same portmap works almost fine with glibc-2.0.6-9.

I am trying to get an lx164 with RH5.0 up to "production" stability,
but updates still in "pre-beta" state put on the Errata/Updates section
aren't going to help. I understand that the people at RedHat are overworked
and that the alpha test base is of more limited lize than the i386, so
please do not take this bug report as a reproach (am not as mad as I look ;-). 

Yet I'd just like that patches to core packages (like glibc) went through
a more severe test and pre-release phase before being posted among the
official RH updates. RH5.0-alpha has been largely a disappointment so
far on this respect.

