Hello;


I original wrote this for the comp.publish.cdrom.software newsgroup,
given the recent dicussion concerning the life exceptancy of CD-R
media I thought it was worth posting to the axp-list.

As pointed out in the current thread concerning CD-R media and
MO media what is the likely hood that in thirty years you will
have the equipment necessary to read the media. The article
entitled "Whoops, there goes another CD-ROM" points out the same
problem. Librarians, and archivist need to archive not only
the media but the hardware to read the media.

For those of us who have been involved with computers are many
years how many still have 8-inch floppy drives? Anyone else
remember paper tape, mylar tape, RL01's, RL02's, RP07's,
programs stored to cassette tapes (Altair, TI99/4a, etc)

How many could read an older 150 MB cartridge tape?

How many LP's do people still have.

The point of media life expectancy now days requires that
all the hardware be archived with a quantity of spare parts.

The question still remains though:
In 2028 how many people will still be able to operate
a PC from 1998?

-------Begin Original Posting-------

After reading the 16 Feb 1998 issue of U.S.News & World Report entitled
"Whoops, there goes another CD-ROM", I was interested in the graph
provided by the National Media Lab. The graph shows the 
'Storage Life in years' for various media.
Example:

Magnetic Tapes:
Half-inch reel-to-reel tape:
All major brands 5 years; high-quality brands 10+ years.
Half-inch tape cartridges:
All major brands 5 years; high-quality brands 11+ years.
VHS tape:
All major brands roughly 1.5 - 2 years; high-quality brands 10 years.
Quarter-inch tape:
All major brands roughly 1.5 - 2 years; high-quality brands 10 years.

Optical Disks:
CD-ROM (play only):
All major brands 5 years; high-quality brands 50 years
CD-R (recordable CDs):
All major brands roughly 1.5 - 2 years; high-quality brands roughly 30 -
35 years

The graph also lists Microfilm, and Paper.
According to the graph supplied by National Media Lab,
high-quality low acid, buffered paper will last 500 years.
Basically this type of paper outlasts all other media listed.

What neither the article or graph detail was the testing performed
by National Media Labs from which the data for the graph was taken.

A quick search of the Internet located the National Media Lab.

Brief information concerning National Media Lab is given below,
please refer to http://www.nta.org/ntahome.html .

To quote the above page:
<Begin Quote>
The National Technology Alliance is a partnership between government,
industry, and academia to leverage high value information technologies
developed in the commercial world for the benefit of government users.

The National Media Laboratory (NML) hosted by 3M Company.
The National Information Display Laboratory (NIDL) hosted at the Sarnoff
Corporation.
The National Center for Applied Technology (NCAT) hosted at Autometric
Inc.
<End   Quote>

While I have been unable to find the same graph on the Web site, I have
found some rather interesting reading at

http://www.nta.org/Publications/Presentations/PreservationOfDigitalMaterials/
.

<Begin Quote>
Long-Term Preservation of Digital Materials

Dr. John W.C. Van Bogart
National Media Laboratory

Introduction / Digital Storage Media
What Can Go Wrong with Digital Materials?
How Long do Digital Storage Materials Last?
What Can Be Done to Increase Longevity?
<End   Quote>

The site also has a rather good Web Page on

'CD Recording: A Troubleshooting Guide'

located at
 
http://www.nta.org/Publications/TechnicalReports/EducationAndTraining/SIGCATCDRHandbook/

Although the material is from December 1996 it is still relevant today,
in my opinion.

The site also has a rather good Web Page entitled 
"Doculabs Test Report: Compatibility of CD-R Media, Readers, and Writers"
located at

http://www.nta.org/Publications/TechnicalReports/MediaStability/DoculabsCDRCompatability/

The site is worth a visit in my opinion.

The above are my thoughts and opinions, your thoughts, opinions, and
mileage may vary.

