Not much help I'm afraid, just some observations.

I have been unable to get a working compilation of Beta3 on my RH 5.0
system. Basically I got the same results that you did.

I have read on this list that g++ is "broken" under RH 5.0, this would
appear to be the case.

The only way I got KDE Beta3 to run on my RH5.0 system was to use the
RPMs that were created by
        Johannes Aschauer <Hans.Aschauer@Physik.Uni-Muenchen.DE>

These can be found at:

ftp://nostromo.stat.physik.uni-muenchen.de/pub/kde

Hans says he compiled them under 5.0 using EGCS 1.0.1, and that he had
to manually compile some of the files without optimizations in order to
make things work.

So Beta3 is working fine on my machine. The only problem is that he
compiled against QT 1.31, and some of the newer stuff wants 1.32. Also,
some of the newer stuff (notably klyx) will not run with the Beta3 libs
and require libs from a more recent snapshot.

Hans posted a "semi-statically" linked klyx that he thought would work
with a stock Beta3 installation but I have so far been unable to make it
work.

Obviously we need to be able to compile things ourselves. Is g++ really
broken under 5.0 (it would seem to be) and is EGCS a solution to this
problem? (likely, since the RPMs work, but not completely since I tried
it under 1.0.2). Most importantly, is RedHat working on this problem?

I installed EGCS 1.0.2 and ran the test suite, 98% of the "torture test"
programs compiled OK.

BTW - if anybody is trying to compile the KDE stuff on an SX-based Alpha
machine, I had to change the references to alphaev56 to alphapca56 in
the config.sub files (two places each) to make things even get started.
Apparently the 21164pc CPU reports its identity differently from the
21164a.

There don;t seem to be many people running Alphas on the KDE mailing
lists. I get responses like "we don't know what's happening on the
Alphas". Maybe I should just ship them my old UDB ? :-)

Anyway, that's what I know so far, I'm still working on it :-)


-- 
Brian E.W. Wood  beww@intac.com


