"M. Nicklaus" <mn1@helix.nih.gov> writes:

> Following up on my previous posts concerning the max. number
> of open files per process.  This limit is 256 by default in
> Linux (set in /usr/src/linux/include/linux/fs.h and limits.h
> ---does any of these two files take precedence if NR_OPEN
> differs between them, BTW?)
> 
> In DU, the max. number of open files per process is 4096.
> Since we need to run the same code on both platforms, I want
> to increase NR_OPEN (and the other related limits such as
> NR_FILE, NR_INODE) 16-fold and rebuild the Linux kernel.
> 
> Before I get myself into trouble:  Has anyone done this
> (or a similarly significant increase) successfully?

It's been discussed on the linux-kernel mailing list.  Try searching
for max open files on:

   http://www.uwsg.indiana.edu/search/AT-linux_kernel_mailquery.html.

In particular, there's a patch and a mod for the patch at:

   http://www.uwsg.indiana.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/9612.1/0037.html

There's an explanation of how to modify the max # of open files on the
system (as opposed to max open files per process) while the system is
running:

   http://www.uwsg.indiana.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/9611.1/0100.html

The above leads to a patch & discussion at:

   http://www.linux.org.za/filehandle.patch.linux

The above includes dynamic allocation of some memory, presumably so
that each process doesn't suck up more memory due to the larger
limits.

There's also a warning about how *not* to go about it at:

   http://www.uwsg.indiana.edu/hypermail/linux/kernel/9701.0/0058.html

-- 
Harvey J. Stein
Berger Financial Research
hjstein@bfr.co.il


