On Wed, 8 Apr 1998, Dave Tucker wrote:
>
>      Hence, I would like to ask that someone in the know please summarize 
>      the connector problem, or hypothesize why my system board is 
>      frequently going south.

	What I did when this started happening to me was to take apart the
power connector that attached to the motherboard. I popped out the two
green connectors, and found them almost black with corrosion. I simply took
a screw driver and scrapped off the corrosion. Not the greatest solution,
but quick and worked for me. I would suggest steel wool, or some type of
corrosion removing solvent, probably gentler than a screwdriver. :) I
then reassembled the connector, plugged it into the motherboard, and no
problems in four months. 
	Though I also placed two 12V fans (running at ~7V each) on top of
the air vents where the UDB is sitting upright in its stand. These fans
run 24 x 7, and keep the entire case of the UDB significantly cooler. They
are currently hooked up to an external power supply I built in an
Electrical Lab class at school. This summer I plan to build a bit more
self contained unit. IMHO, the fans help the system out a lot, and should
keep heat problems further and fewer between. 
	My two cents, hope they help.

