We recently also had a problem with our UDB, which I guess we still do.
It died primarily due to bad memory, but upon dissection we found that the
power supply plastic connector has turned brown and gets unusually hot.
Removing the memory solved the problem so we figured all was well if not a
little strange.  Turned it on and it booted.  Swell, we'll push the gut
back into the case.  The UDB dies.  D'oh!  Take the guts out, let it cool,
push them back in.  Wait a few minutes (worked for the Sun 4/330).  Turn
it on, all is well.  Finishes fsck'ing the drives and down it goes.  :(
Once more, haul out the guts and turn it on.  It boots!

So now our UDB is sitting on the floor half disassembled.  It seems that
it is more important to keep the power supply connector cool than it is to
keep the CPU cool, so it sits in the open A/C.

Hmm.  I was going to tell you the uptime, but it looks like the machine is
down.  I guess it's time to move everything over to the SPARC ... or the
