Another possible solution:
The heat described in these messages, and the resulting carbonization, is
usually a symptom of 2 things:
1) Poor connection / conductivity causing heat and related effects
2) A possibility of poor soldered connections where the board seats in the
motherboard.

To eliminate the first, I could recommend 2 possible solutions:
The first involves a commercial product called "Tweak" which one uses to
coat the surface of connactor parts to improve conductivity. It can be
found at some of the better electronics stores. In my experience it can
help a lot. Back in the Atari ST days we used to use it to eliminate a
problem where the machines would unexpectedly stop working. We used to use
the practice of picking up the machine and giving it a twist (the "Atari
Twist"). Uing Tweak on all the connectors eliminated this.

For the second, it is quite easy to reove the board and reheat/solder the
connsctor from the underside of the motherboard.

Another, more aggressive solution is to replace the faulty connectors, by
installing a new connector. The power connectors used on the UDB are
similar to ones used in P.C. motherboards, and if one were to ask at your
favourite local PC repair shop I suspect getting them to give you an old
dead 486 board would not be difficult. Maybe $5 would persuade them at
worst..   ;^}
If you want to order new power connectors for the board the part is a Molex
87218-1202.

Ultimately I think the real cause of most of these problems is running the
poor beasties at high clock rates. The Delta power supplies in these are
labelled as providing 7A at 3.3V and if we look up specs for a 21066 CPU we
can see the following:
166MHz:	3.3V	4.31A
233MHz:	3.3V	6.43A
266MHz:	3.3V	8.64A

While the 300MHz rating isn't given (it's not a "legal" value for this
chip) we can easily extrapolate that it should be drawing around 11 to 13A
of 3.3V, which is far more than this puny power supply was ever intended to
produce. The fact that it runs at all is a testimony to decent quality.
Unfortunately the heat produced at critical point like connectors is the
long term problem.

A suggestion:

If enough people want to get a longer term solution for heat problems, the
easiest solution I can see is to have a new, taller cover (top) for the UDB
fabricated, with 2 AC fans in it.
I expect that if there are at least 10 people who want it I can arrange for
these to be made at a cost of about $60 each, including 2 ball-bearing 90mm
AC fans. I suggest AC rather than DC fans because we don't want to increase
the power supply output load. We can supply a male plug to conenct to the
AUX connector at the back of the case.

This cover would increase the thickness of the UDB by about 1.5", and be
fabricated out of steel with a powder-epoxy grey finish. While not as
elegant looking as the original, it would be decent enough, and would also
incorporate a mount bracket for a second hard drive.

Let me know if this interests you. If I get 10 or more candidates I will
get the pieces made.

Have a good Easter weekend all.



