Uh, where were you guys when I posted the attached (at the very end) nearly
a month ago?

I had a grand total of 3 people respond, 2 with SX's that my suggestions
worked for, and one with a MIATA (which also was fixed :-).

I assumed, from that response, that either no others were having problems,
or that their problems had been fixed, so I didn't follow up any further.

Anyway, let's go on...

>>> Christian Worley said:
> >runs approx 2x fast in both Linux & NT. :(
> 
> I had an approx. 2x speedup problem (in fact, I've had it more than
> once!).  they were always due to bad milo compiles.  It's burned me so
> many times that I always check the clock before doing any benchmarks.

Well, the 2x under NT certainly isn't because of a bad MILO... ;-}

The double- or half-speed clock problems first came to my notice (and to
axp-list's) with the LX164; seems that the AlphaBIOS coders had written the
early releases (at least) with a clock tick of 512/sec, contrary to the
1024/sec that all our previous boxes had provided. Thus you'd see twice the
expected BogoMIPs and the clock would appear to be running at half speed,
mainly because it *was*; MILO didn't muck about with RTC settings, then.

Early on, I modified older PALcode for the LX164 to do correct initialization
of the RTC, and this fixed that problem for that platform.

Later, in preparing the MILOs I built for inclusion in RH 5.0, I built LX
and SX versions from all new PALcode sources which, for whatever reason, had
had the expected RTC initialization code removed, from their PALcode. Early
releases of those MILOs, and I think SX164 was one of them, actually were
made available without the clock init code. However, this oversight was
rectified, I believe, in all the MILOs that were included in RH 5.0. Hence
my posting's initial statement of the things to try to determine if it was
indeed a bad MILO that was the cause.

That's not to say that all the problems you've been describing can be
explained by a bad MILO. Certainly, if something really was fixed by a new
motherboard with all the others things staying the same, I'd be hard pressed
to offer a sane explanation... ;-}

Bottom line: chances are good if you see a BogoMIPS of approximately twice
the MHz of the processor, you're seeing the half-speed problem that my fixes
may help.

BogoMIPS of half the MHZ would indeed mean a double-speed clock, which is
quite possibly a symptom of a completely different problem, epecially if
still seen using those fixed components mentioned below. Please, contact me
directly if you see this happening: <Jay.Estabrook@digital.com>.

> As I recall, on RH4.2, don't recompile the PALCODE (use what's in the
> distribution), and on RH5.0 DO recompile the PALCODE when rebuilding
> milo.

Actually, it's the DEC BIOS emulator that you use in 4.2 and not in 5.0.
And you SHOULD REOMPILE the SX and LX PALcode IN EITHER CASE (4.2 or 5.0) as
the "canned" versions are stale and/or do not contain the clock init code
described above.

> I still see clocks that gain or loose 10 seconds an hour, with the
> above fixes, that I cannot explain.

I think I can; I call this the "drift" problem, to differentiate it from the
"half-speed" problem we've been talking about. It affects LX, SX, and MIATA,
and fixing it involves using updated PALcode. Suffice it to say that the
PYXIS core logic on those motherboards has some signal propagation delays
that sometimes result in the clock interrupt being taken twice, because the
clearing signal hadn't reset the state before the interrupt code returned to
lower or zero IPL.

I'm unsure as to which of the MILOs I built for RH 5.0 actually had the
"drift correction" code active. I believe that the latest MILOs in the
/pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/ directory on gatekeeper.dec.com, ie those
that have appeared recently or since the November release, should have that
code present and active. However, if you'd like to build it for yourselves,
the following code ought to appear in the Real Time Clock interrupt handler,
usually labelled Int22Handler in the palcode/*/platform.S file in the MILO
sources. The "drift" fix looks like (only the code inside the #if 1 block):

        OutPortByte(RTCADD,0x0C,p6,p7)  // Set up RTCADD to index register C.
        InPortByte(RTCDAT,p6,p7)        // Read to clear interrupt.
#if 1
        // this shouldn't hurt anything, but... :-\
        
        srl     p6, 6, p6               // Check for real interrupt.
        blbc    p6, DismissInterrupt    // Nope, just exit.
#endif
        mtpr    p4, excAddr             // Load kernel entry address.
        bis     zero, INT_K_CLK, a0     // Signal Real Time Clock interrupt.

PLEASE, this is only applicable to LX164, SX164, and MIATA.

Good luck, and please let me know what you find out.

--Jay++

