"Michael J. Donahue" <donahue@ulexite.nist.gov> writes:

> Joakim Roubert asks:
> >On Tue, 14 Apr 1998, Michael J. Donahue wrote:
> >
> >> FWIW, I have found that I can get 115200 if I use 'setserial' with
> >> spd_vhi, and then don't specify a speed in my dip script. 
> >
> >What kernel?
> 
> I don't have access to that machine at the moment, but it should be
> the kernel that ships with RH 4.2.  Maybe 2.0.30?
> 
> Jay A Estabrook writes:
> >From the "setserial" manpage:
> >
> >       spd_hi Use 57.6kb when the  application  requests  38.4kb.
> >              This parameter may be specified by a non-privileged
> >              user.
> >
> >       spd_vhi
> >              Use 115kb when  the  application  requests  38.4kb.
> >              This parameter may be specified by a non-privileged
> >              user.
> >
> >So, it looks like you *should* set the speed in the dip script to 38.4K.
> >Of course, you may not need to if this is somehow the default... ;-}

My understanding was that this setserial 'spd_vhi' kludge was only
necessary because of limitations in the old termios (was it termio then?)
struct which couldn't take a number that high.
The newer kernels use struct termios which uses an unsigned int for speed_t
so shouldn't have that limitation.

My Intel machine running 2.0.33 (libc5) doesn't need to use the spd_vhi
kludge, and will happily accept 115200 for pppd or minicom. I assumed that
an Alpha running 2.0.33 would do the same.

Of course I could be entirely wrong!


