Oh boy, what fun..  As noted by Greg Lindahl, someone (since identified as
Hugo van der Kooij - THANK YOU!!) previously posted how to go about getting
2.0.33 up on Alphas.

As I understand it (with at least some confirmation via running thru the
'make config' several 10s of times - yes, I have ALWAYS used 'make config',
never 'make menuconfig'), most/many of the kernel distributions
have Makefiles config'ed for Intel machines and they have to be patched for
the Alpha arch.  regardless, the kernel tree has to be patched for Alphas.

Hugo has described this in a posting that can still be seen at:

http://archive.redhat.com/axp-list/1998-February/0319.html

but since I've gone thru it to patch the kernel on my 533MHz LX164 (which
incidentally works fine with the original RH5-distributed MILO built vs 2.0.30
- something that I also helped muddy the waters regarding - sorry, Jay), I've
re-tarred the source tree and made it available at:

ftp://mamba.bio.uci.edu/pub/Linux-Alpha/linux-2.0.33-patched-for-Alpha.tar.gz

This patching inserts the necessary lines to config for the
CONFIG_ALPHA_LX164 etc that mystified me before.  I assume that there is
already an FTP distribution site for this patched version, which is why
Michal Jaegermann could not fail to miss that config, but I d/l'ed ~7
kernels from different places and read a fair # of docs trying to find
this info, and could not.

I tried this once on my machine and it worked fine with MY set of configs,
but it has not been extensively tested otherwise.  Caveat Downloader!

This 2.0.33 HAS been quite a bit more robust than the 2.0.30 that we were
using before, tho - allowing at least 2 problematic simulations to run to
completion (2.5 times as fast as on a 266 MHz PII).

Hope this is of use to someone.

Cheers,
Harry

