There are a couple of commercial compilers:
NAG supposedly has a FORTRAN "compiler" for the alpha -- I think what it 
really is, is a FORTRAN to C translater, the output of which is to be compiled
with gcc.  Microway has a FORTRAN compiler, which I bought with a system from
them, but found to be less than ideal.  I think you have to do a lot of hand-
coding with their compiler and it does not have some of the nice features and
options of a "good" compiler, such as DEC's FORTRAN.  The documentation for
their compiler seemed to be general document for all their platforms (DOS,
i860, alpha), but did not differentiate too well as to what applied to which
platform.  I had their older 32bit and their newer 64bit versions.  The code
we got never performed as well as code from DEC's compiler.  Independent of
their compiler, I like the system (hardware) I got from Microway -- I upgraded
it to DEC UNIX and DEC FORTRAN.  I think if you want to do FORTRAN, I'd 
recommend getting DEC UNIX + DEC FORTRAN.



