Greg Lindahl wrote:
> 
> > I've heard varying opinions about their Alpha Linux compilers (and have
> > never used them myself), but Microway claims to have a Fortran77 Compiler for
> > Linux:
> >
> > http://www.microway.com/ndpfort.html
> >
> > priced at: US$1200
> 
> I attempted to go around and get all the Alpha and Intel Linux
> compiler companies to give me a copy so I could run SPEC95
> head-to-head with all of them, and Microway is one of the companies
> that hasn't replied. Given that they haven't published any SPEC95
> numbers, you shouldn't assume they are any better than g77-egcs; they
> probably use the system libraries, which is egcs's current largest
> weakness.
> 

It may be a weakness in your code but it isn't in many others.
I do signal processing with egcs g77 and have no problems with
it and as such recommend it. While DEC UNIX is an option for those
that can afford it (DEC UNIX is around $1000-1500 and the DEC
F77 compiler is around $2000 last I priced it). So, you can buy
DEC SW or an additional machine. You need to decide which is more
valuable to you, the SW or another system. I decided the system
was more valuable for my work.


-- 
Regards,

Wes


