I've encountered a bad drive this way.  I suggest wiping the partitions
and formatting to FAT in Windows and doing an exhaustive check from there.
I assume you have access to a Windows station with SCSI.  When I did this
test, it locked in about the same place.  By the difference in the OS
in use, I assumed that the disk must be bad.  Having a copy of Windows on
hand makes hardware diagnostics more comprehensive for pinpointing
problems.  I have stuff that works in Linux but not NT (CD changer) and NT
and not so well in Linux (RAM).  I suggest having a drive light available
to see if there is any activity.

