With regards to your message at 08:44 AM 04-17-98 -0400, Ken Hansen. Where
you stated:
>Hello,
>	I am considering the purchase of a "Building Block" system
>(MB/CPU/PS/CASE) for a 533 Mhz 164SX, and I was wondering what to
>expect performance wise. My anticipated system would be:
>
>	164SX @ 533 Mhz w/1 MB cache
>
>	NCR Ultra SCSI (forget model # - 860?)
Probably 875. I recommend the Intraserver for best Alpha support.

>	2 4GB Ultra Narrow SCSI drives
Narrow doesn't save much in $ over wide, and a noticable performance
difference is there.

>	128 Meg RAM
>
>	#9 4 Meg PCI video (64GXE Pro I think)
Which video chip? How about X support? Maybe consider an ATI or MAtrox
Millenium II.

>Should this system be 2x 233 Mhz noname/UDB, 4X, other? It is for
>a general desktop machine, and I am hoping for a BIG increase in
>performance over my noname & UDB - will I be let down? What kind
>of Pentium would such a system "feel like" - a 233 Mhz?
More like a Pentium II - 300MHz if bnchmarks are to believed. And this is
just the raw integer side of things. If you take proper advantage of the
superior memory I/O and bus performance you may see even better figures.
And if floating point performance matters in your application, then about
the equivalent of a Pentium II - 400MHz.

Real world compile time on a UDB 166MHz for Linux 2.0.33 kernel = about 52
minutes.
On a 233MHz "noname" with 1MB cache this is about 38 minutes.
On a PC64+ 275MHz with 2MB cache it is about 17 minutes.
On a PC164 500MHz it is about 10 minutes.
On an LX it is about 4 to 5 minutes.
Anybody care to post compile times for the SX?

>	Also, What am I missing by not going with a 164LX - the cost
>is aprox. 2/3 more, and performance increase appears to be on the order
>of 15 - 20%?

Shouldn't be that big a price difference. Price diff between boards should
run about $300 or less nowadays.
There is a small premium for the ECC RAM the LX uses over the 64bit non-ECC
you can use in the SX, about $150 for 128MB. And the ECC offers some
benefits. Also, for those who like to do this sort of thing the LX can
happily run overclocked at 600, while the SX can not.

Specmarks, (courtesy of an earlier post from Stephen Gaudet):

PC164LX & SX
Processor     cache   	SPECint95  	SPECfp95
211PC-533MHz  1mb-7.5ns	12.4		16.1 <SX>
21164-533MHz  2mb-9ns	16.1		18.8 <LX>
21164-600MHz  2mb-9ns	17.9		19.9 <LX>

And, to take this farther:
21164-600MHz  4mb-7ns   18.9        20.4 <LX>

As you can see on this report integer performance at 533MHz is 30% better
for LX (16.1/12.4), and 44% better at 600MHz (17.9/12.4).

Beyond that I am told that if you run more CPU and RAM intensive jobs, such
as compiling the larger cache on the LX makes for a noticable performance
difference.
  


