On Sat, 18 Apr 1998, Pablo A. Godel wrote:

> I founded this  at /var/log/messages
> 
> Apr 18 18:55:24 alpha kernel: netstat(5403): unaligned trap at
> 00000155556b901c:  000000011ffff914 2d 10
[...]

> What does it mean??

Basically, it means that the code for netstat (and/or the program used to
compile it) is rather poor. I really don't know all the details, but what
is basically happening is that the Alpha is aligned for 64 bit data, and
it is getting data (and/or instructions) that are not 64-bit aligned.
They may be 32-bit (as this is common) or something entirely different.
On the Alpha, this means a performance decrease to fix-up the bad
alignment.  Since it is a relatively 'simple' fix (recompile/rewrite) it
complains and logs the trap.  Otherwise, it is harmless.

