On Sat, 18 Apr 1998, Maurice Hilarius wrote:

> Real world compile time on a UDB 166MHz for Linux 2.0.33 kernel = about 52
> minutes.
> On a 233MHz "noname" with 1MB cache this is about 38 minutes.
> On a PC64+ 275MHz with 2MB cache it is about 17 minutes.
> On a PC164 500MHz it is about 10 minutes.
> On an LX it is about 4 to 5 minutes.
> Anybody care to post compile times for the SX?

313.664u 21.620s 5:56.70 93.9%  0+0k 0+0io 171465pf+0w

This compile includes ncr scsi, ide, one ethernet driver etc. and was done
with egcs-1.0.1.

I tend to compare my SX with PPro 200, and I think they are about equal
in compiling larger projects. If I compare memory bandwith and running
programs with smaller footprint than gcc then the alpha wins with great
marigin.

Some examples:

mp3 encoding (fp intensive). A test file with 30 seconds of music takes 37
seconds to encode on the SX and it takes 90 seconds on the PPro 200.

DES cryptation (integer, small footprint). I had about 6,000,000 keys per
second on my SX and the PPro did about 900,000. I think a PII-300 did
about 1,500,000 keys/sec.

Quake (fp intensive, but big footprint) is a little quicker on the SX
than the PPro, a fps test at the same place gives 105 fps on the SX and 90
pfs on the PPro. You should take into count that the intel version has
some assembler optimizations while all code is compiled from C on the
alpha.

I think that you won't be disappointed in a 164SX, but if the price
difference is small and you want to compile a lot a LX should be 
considered.

Oh, I have tested the memory bandwidth a little too and I must say I'm
pretty impressed. Here are my test results for the L2 cache:

memset() speed: 2002.2 Mb/s
memcpy() speed: 902.9 Mb/s

This test was done by timing 64k blocks with 8192 repetitions.

Interrestingly, write speed (memset) and copying speed (memcpy) is
about equal to external RAM:

memset() speed: 211.3 Mb/s
memcpy() speed: 206.3 Mb/s

This test was done by timing 2MB blocks with 256 repetitions.

The same tests were applied on the PPro 200 (running NetBSD), but I didn't
get any resonable numbers for cache speed (the indication for the SX
cache speed might be incorrect too). I think the memory speed is correct
though, and I know the memcpy speed was better (more like the memset
speed) when the machine was running Linux. Here are the results for
memory speed:

memset() speed: 76.4 Mb/s
memcpy() speed: 50.8 Mb/s

/Andreas


