On Fri, 17 Apr 1998, S. Daniel wrote:

> How do I get an XL300 to boot from the floppy? Is this in a FAQ somewhere?
> Thanks.
> 
I setup Red Hat 5.0 just a couple of days ago, and it wasn't exactly like 
the instructions in the Installation Guide, but it was close.

Make your three floppies - Milo, boot and root.  Use the milo image 
xlt.img under milo/images, i.e., milo/images/xlt.img on the Red Hat 5.0 CD, 
the boot image images/xlt.img and the root image in images/ramdisk.img.  
Actually, I downloaded the latest ramdisk.img from the Red Hat 5.0 update 
directory.  Use dd as described in the Red Hat Installation Guide to make 
these diskettes.

Now, setup the OS selection:

When you boot, press F2 to get to the AlphaBIOS setup screen.  Select 
'Utilities', the 'OS Selection Setup ...'  In this screen, press the 
INSERT key to create a new OS entry.  Give it a name such as "Linux 
Floppy".  Press TAB to get to the next field.  Under boot file, press '+' 
until you see 'A:', then press TAB to get to the next field on that 
line.  Enter '\linload.exe' (without the single quotes).  Press TAB to 
get to the "OS Path:" line.  It doesn't matter what you put here.  I 
pressed '+' until "Disk 0, Partition 1" was there and entered '\' 
(without the single quotes) for the path.  That just makes sure you do 
not get an error when AlphaBIOS checks the paths.  Do not put anything in 
the "OS Options" field.  Put your MILO diskette into the diskette drive, 
then press ENTER to exit the new OS entry screen.  It should test the 
entries.  Then you press F10 to save the OS selection.  Press ENTER to 
confirm it, then ESC twice to get to the operating system selection 
screen.  You should see 'Linux Floppy' as one of your selections, perhaps 
your only selection if you have not installed Windows NT.

Now, put the highlight on the 'Linux Floppy' item and (with your MILO 
floppy in the floppy drive) press ENTER.

You should see a bunch of informational messages and after about 15 - 20 
sec. you should see a MILO> prompt.

Now, remove the MILO diskette and put the boot diskette in the floppy 
drive.  Enter at the MILO> prompt:

boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1

and press ENTER.  You should see a lot of informational messages, then a 
bunch of hash marks, then a message to put your root floppy disk in the 
floppy drive.  Remove the boot diskette, put in the ramdisk diskette, and 
press ENTER.

After the ramdisk loads, you should see the Red Hat installation screen.

I hope this is useful - It worked for me.  The hard part was figuring out 
exactly what to put in the OS selection screen when the Linux Floppy OS 
was setup.

Good luck.

Andy Seila

