On Apr 20, adisaacs@trurl.resnet.mtu.edu wrote:

> On Sun, Apr 19, 1998 at 10:12:12PM -0400, Uncle George wrote:
> > my X setver XF86_SVGA for the alpha port gets sig 11 when i run 2 java
> > applications. I'm fed up.
> > 
> > HOW DOES ONE DEBUG THE X SERVER????
> 
> The best way is either over a network, or via a serial terminal.  Grab an
> XFree86 source tree from ftp.xfree86.org, compile it (with debugging
> symbols, preferably), and gdb away.  Don't try this on console, though.

compiling the whole X server with `-g' isn't necessary nor usefull,
this will only create _huge_ binaries and quires even more memory in gdb.

first check the trace back of a core dump or run the normal X server once
in gdb from remote login or similar.  then just compile the last few routines
in the traceback with `-g' (or the directories including them, e.g.
..../xfree86/vga256/drivers/s3v for SVGA/s3_virge) and link a new server binary.
usual commands are:

	cd ..../xfree86/vga256/drivers/s3v
	rm *.o
	make "CDEBUGFLAGS=-O2 -g"                #  check original settings in Makefile!!
	make -C .. SUBDIRS=s3v

	cd ..../xc/programs/Xserver
	make loadXF86_SVGA


Harald
