I think it's easier to just start X as you normally do. Then, telnet in from
another system and use gdb to attach to the X process. Tell gdb to
then continue running the program and go back to your system and
cause the error. Everything should die but if you go back to the gdb
session on the other screen, you'll have the info you need. I did it
for the 20 windows problem and it worked. I downloaded the latest
Xserver code (3.3.2) and built it with '-g' added to the compile
flags to see if it's possible to trace where that problem goes belly
up in the X server. The SIGSEGV is at:

ProcChangeSaveSet(), at dispatch.c:473

    465     pWin = (WindowPtr)SecurityLookupWindow(stuff->window, client,
    466                                            SecurityReadAccess);
    467     if (!pWin)
    468         return(BadWindow);
    469     if (client->clientAsMask == (CLIENT_BITS(pWin->drawable.id)))
    470         return BadMatch;
    471     if ((stuff->mode == SetModeInsert) || (stuff->mode ==
SetModeDelete))
    472     {
    473         result = AlterSaveSetForClient(client, pWin, stuff->mode);
<---- Near here!!!
    474         if (client->noClientException != Success)
    475             return(client->noClientException);
    476         else
    477             return(result);
    478     }
    479     else
    480     {
    481         client->errorValue = stuff->mode;
    482         return( BadValue );
    483     }

Haven't figured out exactly what broke yet but at least I know
where to look. If anyone else knows more about the X servers,
maybe they could also look at this area to see what might be
wrong. One other thing. The binary with '-g' on all source is
about 17MB so you need a system with lots of memory to
do this for the full server source.


