On Wed, 22 Apr 1998, Todd Fleisher wrote:
> 	I am trying to install Redhat via FTP on my 166 UDB. However, I have been unable to get it to see the network. In the Redhat setup, I get the following log messages:
> 	* eth0 is available -- using it for networking
> 	<4> eth0: media is TP.
> 	yadda, yadda, yadda
> 	* bootpc: gethostbyaddr No such file or directory
> 	* reverse name lookup failed

There are many things I don't know about, but I have succeeded in
instualling RH5.0 on my UDB via FTP, several times over.  Are you sure you
have a valid DNS server ip?  Use one that is in existence at the time
of install (ie. don't set the IP to what your UDB will use, as in DNS).
If you have to setup the computer name and the secondary / tertiary 
nameserver ip, then you don't have a valid lookup yet.  Your machine
should be entered in the database of your primary DNS machine.

Like I said, I'm not sure what your error stems from, as I've never had
that particular error, and I don't know everything about networking
except that I usually can get mine to work nicely. Also, make sure your
cable/cord works and is seated properly in all respective sockets.  I also
suggest that you try to see if WinNT/Alpha works the network, if only to
run it temprorily, because NT's networking is a bit more fool proof.  This
is how I found a problem on the coax.

I apoligize for the above sacrelige, but it's true, unless you are a Linux
guru, of which I am not, at least not yet - maybe this summer I'll catch
up more on Linux!

