"Adrian E. Feiguin" <adrian@ifir.ifir.edu.ar> writes:
> I'm sorry. I've been loosing some mail lately. If I well understand
> Netscape was compiled using the Qt libraries, is that right? Is it
> working? I downloaded Mozilla's sources and installed Qt, what's next?
> I read something like there are some patches needed, how can I get them?
> (Uncle George's working on this?)

OK, since I announced that I compiled qtscape and that	"it basically
works" let me comment on this.

Uncle George posted some patches to this list last week, which you
could use to compile netscape with lesstif-current. Now, as I didn't
had lesstif-current at home, but the QtScape source, I tried to 
apply them to QtScape. It turned out that they had been mangled by
the mail, but it was easy to do the patches by hand.

After fixing to little additional problems, I got QtScape
running. Since somebody had asked before, if anybody tried it, it
annouced that.

BUT: QtScape runs as good or as bad as it does on other OSs. That's 
rather bad, espeacially if frames are involved. Keep in mind that 
it was a very, very quick port. There are missing features and a lot
of bugs due to the qt-frontend part of QtScape.

You'll be better off with getting lesstif-current. This has bugs to,
but that's mostly related to brocken menu's and such. The browser
display itself, including frames, works much better than with QtScape.

I hoped this clarified things.

For Uncle George's patches, pointers to QtScape, Netscape, and
lesstif-current try the archive of this list at www.redhat.com,
and/or 'archie'.

Martin 
