> > >I got a error:   VFS:Cannot open root device 03:02
> > >Kernel panic:  VFS: Unable to mount root fs on 03:02

03:02 is /dev/hda2.   Messages above indicate that you are attempting
to boot your system from a non-existent device.  This is not likely to
work.  Where is your real root device?  Documentation describes
a boot command line which is passing this information to a kernel
you attempt to boot.

> > >to solve this  problem??   By the way, how can I turn on the ipfwadm on
> > >RedHat?? Is it already included in the ReedHat system?? 5.0  or 4.2
                                             ^^^
                                       Nice variation! :-)

> > >version?? Or I still have to re-compiler the  kernel??

It is included - a required kernel support in a source form.  You have to
recompile your kernel.  Running a "generic" kernel, which was composed to
boot possibly the biggest number of popular hardware configurations, is
not a good idea anyway - even if you do not need something not included.
It was not designed to be tuned for your hardware.  Besides various pieces
you are likely to want, like vfat support - for example, are missing.

> 
> I suspect you need to turn on loop device, RAM disk and initial
> RAM disk support.

Nope!  None of these is required.  Most definitely not INITRD as it
will be not really used on Alpha.  You have milo.  This is a
"voodoo school" of a system administration.

Loop device and ramdisk are sometimes useful, especially when you are
hacking your ramdisk floppy which you use for an initial booting but
in "normal" kernels they can be compiled as modules.  This, obviously,
does not apply to a kernel you use to load your ramdisk image from
a floppy or you will have a nice "chicken-or-egg" problem.  Other than
that you do not need to lock your kernel memory for things unused.
Remember that a memory used by kernel is not swapable (you would not
want to swap out your swap code, wouldn't you :-).

   Michal


