I am converting a program from FORTRAN to C that reads a set of
keyed input records from an ASCII file, and writes the data
to a binary file. The keys may appear on more than one record with
different types of data. My program stores the key and data in a
structure and writes the structure to a binary file. The program
keeps a list of known keys and their record number in the binary file.

When I use the "high-level" file I/O functions, the program fails.
When I use the "low-level" functions, the program works.

I posted this problem to a couple of comp.os.linux.? newsgroups,
but got no response - maybe no-one else has this problem, or maybe
no-one believes me, or maybe no-one knows how to fix it!

I wrote a test program to demonstrate the problem - see link below -
and have discovered the following:

  The record numbers used by the program are correct.
  The file position (used by fseek) is correct. I also used
    ftell to verify the file position.
  Even though the program reports the correct file position for writing,
      it appends the data to the end of the file (?????).

I have a web page with more details and the sample program at:
http://walleye.gonefishing.sk.ca/laz/rdwr/problem.html

I would appreciate any advice or help to solve this problem.

Larry Z.



