On Mon, 27 Apr 1998, Dan Roscigno wrote:

> 
> I am using the SRM console on some MicroWay 164-LX machines. 
> The system disk is a 9 gig SCSI drive (installed by MicroWay
> and working fine) and I am adding an additional 18 gig SCSI
> drive.
> 
> When I type "fdisk /dev/sda" fdisk does not get all of the
> partitions, I am told that this is a problem with fdisk and
> SRM, and that I should use minlabel.  
> 
> When I use "minlabel /dev/sdb" (/dev/sdb is the new 18 gig
> drive) I am told that the disk needs a default label.  I
> allowed it to write the label, but it has the wrong number
> of cylinders etc.  Do I need an /etc/disktab file for the
> disk, or does minlabel use something else? 

 Linux does not use drive's geometry -- it uses linear sector addresses
and C/H/S is used only within `fdisk' to retain compatibility with
different OSes in the Intel world.  If you need the values of C/H/S
anyway, you may set them within `fdisk' using the <x> "extra
functionality" menu.

 In your situation (Alpha + SRM) you just need to run `fdisk' and type <b>
"edit bsd disklabel".  Then you will be able to create a disklabel similar
to the one of DGUX.

 Alternatively you may use `minlabel' if you prefer to, but my experience
shows that it's easier to obtain the same results within `fdisk'.

