Hey folks, here's a simple trick that generally works on Alpha systems
running the ARC or AlphaBIOS firmware:
In the ARC and Alphabios menus there is generally a utility that allows you
to partition and format FAT filesystems in preparation for installing NT.
  Use this to create a small partition (2 to 3MB is useful, as you may use
this partition to hold linload.exe and milo).
Then, when you go to the Linux fdisk, it will now recognize a perfectly
valid mapping of 64Hx32SPTxNumber of Cylinders. Remeber, actual geometry is
quite irrelevant, as long as the combination of the 3 magic numbers does
not exceed actual disk capacity. As the 64x32xC architecture is the NT
standard, many disk makers now design their drives to exactly fit that
layout, so as to avoid having capacity wasted.
  You may now create your other partitions and swap space and carry on with
installation.



