Chris Price wrote:
> 
> 	Whoa. I was under the impression that Alpha kernels could not use
> modules, and had to be compiled with a monolithic approach.

Your impression was never correct.  It is probably based on a blanket
statement from Red Hat documentation "kernel modules on Alpha do not
work", or something to that effect.  This statement was wrong already
in times of a distribution 4.0 and likely earlier.

It is true that in older times modules required things like 'strip
--strip-debug' and what did not really work was kerneld.  Currently I am
using kerneld as a matter of fact.  The only module with 2.0.33 kernel I
know will not work is nfs.o.  This is due to a mismatch of symbols due
to inlining and can be fixed without big strain but I never bothered.
It is possible that some other module may show similar problems; I did
not try all of them but quite a number.

> I used to
> compile module support in, but always got 'unaligned trap' errors.

See above.  Without modules masquerading would not work and a friend
of mine who is using his Alpha as a masquerading hub for a looong
time would be in a big trouble.

BTW - 'strip --strip-debug' will drop sizes of your modules nowadays
as well and does not harm anything unless you are debugging. :-)

  Michal


