I have a question regarding GCC's compliance with the 64 bit Alpha
architecture.  This came up in conjunction with compiling XPostitPlus,
which casts pointers to int, always a bad idea.  I wrote a simple
program to check data type sizes.  As expected:

	char =		1 byte
	short int =	2 bytes
	int =		4 bytes
	long int =	8 bytes

	pointer =	8 bytes,

Checking further, I took a look in malloc.h, where size_t is defined,
on my Alpha and found

	#define size_t          unsigned int

shouldn't it be

	#define size_t          unsigned long int

on the Alpha???

I thought that the definition of "size_t" was supposed to be the same
size as a pointer (or memory address).  Otherwise, functions that
manipulate memory and/or return pointers, e.g., malloc, won't take
advantage of the available address space.

Jon
