Which version of libraries, headers, etc. you are using?  And are
you sure that you are looking at the right place?  Headers are often
included behind muli-story defines are macro-expansions and their
text likely covers more than one platform.

Doing a real check, which consists of running a program like that:

#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    printf("%ld\n", sizeof(size_t));
    return 0;
}

I see a number 8 (glibc-2.0.7-7 and its headers on Alpha) but AFAIK
it was always like that.

Saying that - to assume in your programs some particular widths of
types, beyond what C standard says about their relative widths,
is a bad practice.  It leads to numerous bugs which later haunt
us on Alpha.  Some programmer knew what these sizes were and now
we have to "port" (translation - fix) this code for Alpha.
Bugs are masked on a platform when assumptions about sizes happens
to be true and the original code is passing for a correct one.

  Michal


