> 
> 
> brinkmann@nmsu.edu said:
> > I thought that the definition of "size_t" was supposed to be the same
> > size as a pointer (or memory address).
> 
> Minor nit:
> 
> The definition of size_t is the type that sizeof returns, and is an
> unsigned integral type. There is no mention if the pointer size in the
> definition of size_t in the C standard that I have in front of me.

That is correct.  But C standard also says that size_t is a return
type of a sizeof operator and about the later C standard says, among
other things, "When applied to an operand that has array type, the
result is the total number of bytes in the array".  In other words,
if you compiler allows for arrays indexed by an 'unsigned long int'
then size_t cannot be too narrow.  Or, if you prefer, a width of
size_t sets an upper limit on a size of any structure, union or array
which your compiler is able to accept.

  Michal


