>>> "Wes Bauske" said:
> 
> Wait a minute. This contradicts what I always thought about the milo/
> kernel relationship. milo is built using a certain level of kernel code.
> It need this to initialize and find disk drives/etc. Once milo loads the
> real kernel, the kernel goes off and figures out for itself where things
> are. I realize milo also loads PAL code and does some HW init that
> is truly unqiue vs the kernel but does this stuff have the potential
> of making the kernel NOT find a HW device??

I wouldn't have thought so, either... :-\

Yes, both MILO and the kernel (normally) do their own PCI bus probing
and set-up. Because the NIC drivers aren't present in MILO, only the
generic probing and (possible) allocation of requested resources (memory,
I/O, and IRQ) is done.

I got involved soon after the time of the original posting, but didn't
(and still don't) have the same card that caused problems. I did, however,
try both a 4-NIC-behind-bridge DEC proto board, and a 2-NIC-behind-bridge
SMC card, and didn't have any problems on the same platform (LX164, IIRC)
with the same MILO.

I'm betting it's the bridge implementation; the two I tried used DEC PCI-PCI
bridges; I have no idea what the poster's card used, nor experience with
anything but the DEC bridges.

> If so, maybe some looking at what PAL code really does is in order.
> Can anyone give an example or two of what PAL code is up to?

PALcode doesn't get involved in PCI stuff, it's all in (IIRC):

	arch/alpha/kernel/bios32.c
	drivers/pci/pci.c

--Jay++

